From 7dba8b7850953968c7773489ecbea39261f48156 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Eaton <weaton@redhat.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 17:07:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] [Core][KV Offload] Free the CPU offload region for a
 process checkpoint

/checkpoint/prepare resets the CPU offloading connector's index but
leaves its region resident and registered with CUDA, so a process
checkpoint carries --kv-offloading-size bytes of dead data. With a
128 GiB region a CRIU image grew from 11.5 GB to 139 GB and restoring
it took longer than a cold boot.

Preparing now stops any background setup still running under
async_init, unregisters the region and frees its pages with
MADV_REMOVE, keeping the mappings so no pointer changes. In the
replicated layout every worker maps one shared region, and pages
another worker still has registered would stay allocated, so the
workers meet at the barrier between unregistering and freeing. A
per-rank region needs no barrier.

Restoring repeats the setup the worker did at startup: in the
background under async_init, where the scheduler turns offloading off at
prepare and back on once every worker reports ready again, or else pre-faulting the same ranges and registering the region again,
failing if the registration does not come back, because the transfer
kernels dereference the host pointers.

OffloadingConnector forwards the connector checkpoint hooks and
supports process checkpoints with the CPU spec. The tiering spec keeps
its memory and does not.

Signed-off-by: Will Eaton <weaton@redhat.com>
Assisted-by: Claude
